  "narrative": {
    "top_frame": "Late-inning rallies are how this team wins now. Fernando Tatis Jr. and Jackson Merrill doing the heavy lifting, then Bogaerts finishing it.",
    "what_this_means": "Tatis went 5-for-5 with two RBIs. That is not a slump night. Merrill went 3-for-5, moving runners along through five and seven when the Padres clawed back into a game they'd handed away in the third. Sixteen hits and eight runs is an offensive evening, full stop. But the lineup didn't build it early. Colorado jumped ahead 4-2 after three innings, and the team chased for four full innings before Tatis and Merrill gave them life in the seventh. King threw six solid innings and Rodriguez leaked two runs in the eighth, so the margin stayed thin. That's when it mattered: Bogaerts walked it off with a sac fly in the ninth, meaning the rally didn't just arrive. It arrived at exactly the moment when one swing could end it. This is a team that doesn't need to lead early anymore because they've learned how to convert late. But eight runners left on base is also a warning. They left two in the third, mild enough. They left two more in the seventh. The formula held because the ninth inning existed. Four wins in a row on games this close. That math gets harder.",
    "what_to_watch": "Gabriel Hughes and the Rockies are back tonight at 1:10 PT. Hughes sits at 2.79 ERA this season and has not allowed more than three runs in his last five starts. The Padres need to break that rhythm early, because late innings won't save them twice.",
